# Chapter 6 — From Uncertainty to an Assembled Universe

*Definite relations can emerge from a reality that was never completely specified in advance.*

The engine in the previous chapter inherited matter, pressure, heat, and reliable relations. We now follow the creation cycle back to its proposed foundation. Ontological uncertainty means that indeterminacy belongs to reality itself; definite physical structures and formal relations emerge together. The account follows their differentiation through symmetry breaking and disequilibrium to the possible assembly of laws and constants. What later appears as a fixed condition may have been an outcome of earlier formation.

This uncertainty is different from not knowing which side of a covered coin faces upward. In that case a definite outcome can exist while an observer lacks the information. Nor does the foundational claim begin with a completed list of universes and a probability attached to each. It proposes that physical realization and its organizing relations become definite together. A probability distribution may describe outcomes of that process, but it must be justified by the process rather than substituted for it.

The associated philosophical claim is that absolute nothingness does not and cannot exist. Removing particles from a theory leaves whatever fields, relations, and possible states that theory permits. An empty container still supplies a place; imagining an event that creates everything already supplies a transition. A quantum vacuum likewise has structure. In the elementary field-theory setting it is a lowest-energy state with properties defined by the theory, not absolute nothingness.[1]

The book treats absolute nothingness as a wholly determinate absence of possibilities, relations, and events, incompatible with its premise of constitutive indeterminacy. This is the proposed ontological foundation of natural emergence. It supplies the starting commitment, while a physical account must explain how definite relationships arise and what they make possible. The claim does not calculate the probability of our universe or require every possible history to reach the same result.[2]

Quantum mechanics gives precise constraints on some physical uncertainties. A position–momentum uncertainty relation concerns the spreads of outcomes associated with a quantum state; it is more than a complaint about imperfect instruments. But successful quantum predictions admit differing ontological interpretations, including Bohm's deterministic formulation. They therefore do not independently establish the foundational premise used here.[3]

The fine-tuning argument follows from that premise through a further hypothesis: as relations become established, they constrain subsequent formation. Some configurations endure, some change the transitions available to others, and some can support the processes that maintain them. Later structures then arise within conditions partly produced by earlier development. The observed world's coordination could be an achievement of that history. It need not be described as a collection of independent settings chosen before anything happened.

This is the deepest application of the joint material and Platonic thesis. The formal side would not be an independently completed catalog of designs waiting for matter. Relations and their physical realization would develop together. The uncertainty is ontological; the proposed assembly process explains how definite organization can arise within it. The natural emergence of a universe concerns the formation of such organization, potentially including spacetime. The assembly of constants concerns explaining the relations and values represented by those constants, rather than assuming them as unexplained settings.

The same premise supplies the book's proposed basis of dialectics. If categorical opposition is ontologically uncertain, the distinctions belonging to one organization need not exhaust the relations that can emerge. Interpenetration of opposites concerns their constitutive dependence. Negation of the negation concerns a transformation that preserves successful relations while reorganizing an exclusive opposition. Quantity into quality concerns the actual transition: changing quantities and constraints make a different physical regime possible. This interpretation connects the ontological foundation to the formation stage of the creation cycle.

The working conjecture is that the universe and its fundamental constants are probably assembled. Here probably expresses a provisional judgment, not a calculated probability. No natural experiment currently identifies the ontology itself. Its physical development would become testable through consequences that distinguish an explicit generative model from competitors. If time belongs to what is assembled, the model must also explain the ordering of development without borrowing an external clock.[4]

Creation from ontological uncertainty leads to the question of differentiation. If definite structure emerges, its formation establishes distinctions: this configuration rather than another, this relation rather than an indefinitely unresolved range. Once a physical description specifies equivalent possibilities, we can ask how an actual arrangement acquires properties that distinguish among them. Symmetry breaking gives that next step a precise form.

Ontological uncertainty is the continuing premise of this account, rather than a trigger that disappears after a first event. The clarified cycle requires a gradient, constrained pathways, and a next organizational level that opens energy access. It then requires autopoiesis to explain recurring organization. Applying that requirement to physical origins is a substantive hypothesis: the productive system, occurrence measure, and regenerative dynamics must be specified. A structure's survival can preserve a condition for later creation, but longevity alone does not demonstrate this stronger continuation claim.

The distinction between a metaphysical foundation and a physical mechanism matters here. A deterministic model can display a bifurcation, and a change of description can occur because earlier knowledge was incomplete. Neither observation by itself establishes ontological uncertainty. The book's stronger proposal is that categorical openness belongs to reality, with the observable transition occurring through specified dynamics. A physical account must derive what changes, why the old regime loses or retains stability, and what new organization becomes accessible.

Consider an idealized model of a magnet. At each site, a small magnetic variable can point in one of two opposite directions. Neighboring variables favor alignment. The rules are unchanged if every direction is reversed. At sufficiently high temperature the system lacks a net preferred direction; below a critical temperature, a large system can develop collective magnetization. The resulting state can have less symmetry than the rule describing its possible behavior.[5]

The example reinforces a distinction used throughout the book. A symmetric rule need not produce a state that displays the entire symmetry of that rule. Particular outcomes require particular states. If two directions are equally allowed, the existence of a magnetized state does not require a law that declares one of those directions universally superior.

This is spontaneous symmetry breaking in an idealized setting. Real samples add boundaries, imperfections, magnetic fields, domains, and finite sizes. Those details matter to which state appears and how long it remains. Their presence does not erase the distinction between the symmetry of the description and the organization of the material.

What has the magnet acquired? It has acquired a collective condition under which subsequent interactions occur. A nearby probe can respond to an organization that cannot be assigned to one constituent considered alone. What became possible depended on the constituents, their interactions, and the conditions under which their collective state formed.

There is no need for an organizing mind outside the material. But there is also no creation from a complete absence of rules. The model already contains the possible directions, interactions, and thermal conditions. It explains how a particular organization arises from those conditions. It does not explain why those are the conditions of existence itself.

A symmetric arrangement is not necessarily a stable arrangement. This distinction can be tested with actual populations of particles. Prepare comparable numbers on two sides of a container and ask whether small departures from equality shrink or grow. Occasional differences occur in many fluctuating systems; the more consequential event is a persistent separation into a crowded side and a sparse side.

In experiments reported in 2001, Ko van der Weele and colleagues used 600 glass beads in a vertically shaken container divided into two equal compartments. Beads could cross the dividing wall. Strong shaking supported an approximately even distribution. Reducing the driving could destabilize that distribution and leave most beads on one side.[6] The apparatus did not need a built-in choice of which side should win.

The mechanism depends on the material. These are macroscopic grains whose collisions dissipate kinetic energy, rather than the ideal elastic particles of an equilibrium gas. A denser compartment has more collisions, slowing its grains and reducing their ability to cross the barrier. A small excess can consequently reduce outward escape enough to draw in still more particles. Jens Eggers developed a quantitative account of this instability and compared it with numerical simulations.[7]

The decisive relation concerns escape rates. If adding particles increases the rate at which they leave, the resulting outflow can restore balance. If adding particles reduces the escape rate in the relevant regime, the same initial excess can grow. Equality remains a possible mathematical solution, but the response to a disturbance has changed. Stability belongs to the dynamics around a state, not merely to the appearance of the state itself.

Mathematicians call a qualitative change in a system's behavior as a condition varies a bifurcation. Here the practical question can be asked without solving an equation: after a slight excess appears on one side, does the resulting exchange remove it or enlarge it? Repeating that perturbation under different shaking conditions locates the change in stability. The measure of imbalance summarizes a collective behavior that would otherwise require following hundreds of separate trajectories.

Turing's account of pattern formation provides another example of this reasoning. Under specified reaction and diffusion conditions, a homogeneous chemical state can become unstable and small disturbances develop into spatial patterns.[8] The resulting pattern changes the local environment in which subsequent reactions occur. This is the book's cumulative argument in a form with definite conditions: a process creates a difference, the difference alters further activity, and a new regime becomes possible. The particular instability must be derived for each system; a grain compartment and a reacting tissue achieve it through different interactions.

An exactly balanced deterministic equation does not have to leave its balanced solution without a perturbation. Real equal-count preparations still contain moving grains, differing velocities, and fluctuations in crossings. They do not specify a perfectly symmetric microscopic history. Once the balanced state is unstable, those small differences can decide which asymmetric outcome develops. In an ideally unbiased set of repetitions, either side can be favored equally often even though individual runs become strongly uneven.

The final populations need not continue diverging without limit. A stationary asymmetric state can balance the number crossing in each direction while retaining unequal numbers on the two sides. Equal flows are a different condition from equal populations. The history has produced an organization in which the same exchange rules sustain a different distribution.

The supply of energy belongs inside this explanation. Shaking sustains motion while collisions transfer energy into other degrees of freedom. Clustering is compatible with dissipation in the complete apparatus; spatial order among the grains is not a demonstration that total thermodynamic entropy has decreased. The experiment also gives a concrete counterexample to the idea that simply increasing the drive must always break symmetry: sufficiently vigorous shaking sustains the balanced regime.

For the book, the important possibility is that differentiation need not be specified as an initial inventory of differences. A material process can amplify a fluctuation, and the resulting distribution changes the conditions of later exchange. The formal symmetry of the arrangement permits equivalent alternatives; the physical dynamics determines whether the balanced state persists. The system is jointly material and Platonic in the sense developed throughout this argument.

This also separates the predictability of a kind of outcome from the predictability of its particular direction. Persistent imbalance can be expected in an unstable regime without a universal preference for left over right. The distinction offers a useful question for our comparison with discovery: could a class of transition become robust while its particular realization remains contingent?

The proposed common driver remains disequilibrium acting through accessible constraints. Here dissipation is part of a demonstrated mechanism of instability. Establishing that the resulting state maximizes an appropriately defined entropy-production rate would require an additional comparison. The experiment gives us a physical route from balance to differentiation, rather than a universal rule that every symmetry must disappear.

The Higgs mechanism brings a related distinction into particle physics, although the magnetic analogy must be handled carefully. The electroweak theory describes a Higgs field whose nonzero vacuum value is associated with the masses of the W and Z bosons; interactions with the field also enter the account of elementary fermion masses. The field's state affects how other fields behave. The 2012 ATLAS and CMS observations of a new boson supplied crucial experimental support for the Higgs picture.[9]

This is not a claim that every particle became massive through the same process, nor that all mass is a gift from a single particle. The underlying theory is also subtler than arrows in a magnet: gauge symmetries include redundancies in description, so different mathematical orientations need not represent physically different worlds. What experiments test are observable consequences, including particles, interactions, and relations among measured quantities.

The relevant lesson is that properties can depend on the state of other physical structure. The account does not begin with a list of independent objects, each carrying all its attributes in isolation. Some attributes are understood through interactions and a background state shared across the theory.

That background raises a further question. How could a physical configuration become a lasting condition for everything subsequently built within it? Even what we call empty space might bear a history of formation and retention.

Imagine a landscape with two valleys separated by a ridge. A ball in the shallower valley can remain there even though a lower resting place exists beyond the ridge. In a field theory, the landscape represents how energy depends on a field's configuration. A local minimum above a deeper one is called a false vacuum. It can be metastable: resistant to small disturbances yet capable of escaping. Quantum tunneling supplies one possible route through the barrier.[10]

Decoherence concerns a different part of this story. Interactions entangle a system with its environment, suppressing observable interference between alternatives in the system's reduced description. Some states retain their correlations more robustly than others. No conscious observer is required. Within cosmology, other fields or modes can provide the environment; nothing outside the universe need watch it. Decoherence helps explain effective classical behavior, but by itself does not settle which interpretation explains an individual outcome.[11]

Under particular conditions, these interactions can also inhibit escape. Thomas Bachlechner's 2013 calculation found that gravitational interactions with thermal radiation could suppress a modeled false-vacuum decay channel. This is a quantum Zeno effect: environmental monitoring impedes a transition. The result depended on the kind of decay considered; it was not a general rule that every environment stabilizes every vacuum.[10]

Robson Christie and colleagues developed a further model in work revised in 2026. They studied a scalar field with two unequal energy minima during inflation, coupled to other fields. Light fields could acquire enhanced occupation of the false vacuum; decoherence then suppressed subsequent tunneling. Crucially, decoherence changed the relative populations little: formation and retention were different processes. Their “cosmic lockdown” still allowed rare crossings over the barrier and concerned a simplified, finite cosmological region.[12]

For this book, the possibility matters because persistence can itself be produced by physical relationships. A configuration need not be the only allowed state, or the lowest-energy state, to become an enduring background for later events. The proposed assembly argument now has a candidate mechanism: formation establishes a configuration, interactions help retain it, and its continued presence constrains subsequent formation. A world can acquire a physical memory of how it developed. This is the sense in which assembly could reach down into the conditions of particle physics rather than begin only with particles already carrying their final properties.

Whether our own vacuum has this history remains a separate question. Assessments of electroweak vacuum stability depend on measured quantities, especially the top-quark mass and strong coupling, and on the assumed physics at higher energies. Research on those dependencies does not establish that decoherence trapped our universe.[13] A surviving metastable state would also require an account of how it was reached, not just why it could remain.

This supplies a candidate condition for the creation cycle rather than a complete instance of it. A trapped field can provide a lasting background for later processes. Under the clarified definition, continuation also requires recurring organization and the autopoietic dynamics producing it. The vacuum models have not supplied that account, nor a comparison establishing greater usable-energy extraction. The cosmic hypothesis must therefore connect retained states to a specified productive organization; it cannot infer self-production simply from a long lifetime.

The argument reaches further than a metaphor, but not yet to a completed origin of laws. These calculations assume fields, interactions, a potential, and spacetime dynamics. They show how a state can be historically established within those assumptions. Explaining why the assumptions themselves exist remains the deeper task. The book therefore proposes that cosmic structure is assembled, with decoherence-assisted retention as one possible part of its physical development. It does not turn a model of vacuum persistence into proof that all constants, or existence itself, have been explained.

The granular and magnetic examples explain how a state can cease to display a symmetry of its governing description. The universe also contains asymmetries involving matter and antimatter, left and right, and past and future. To investigate their common place in a material history, we must identify what changes in each case: a state, an interaction, an abundance, or the conditions under which a process unfolds.

The historical program brings five questions together. Why does the observable universe contain predominantly matter rather than antimatter? Why do weak interactions distinguish left from right? Why does space have its observed three large dimensions? Why do macroscopic processes possess a direction in time? And were physical laws and universal constants themselves assembled?

The book proposes that these asymmetries and structural features reflect historical processes. An apparent initial condition would become an outcome requiring its own material explanation. Symmetry breaking supplies one concrete mechanism by which a physical state acquires distinctions, while the larger program asks whether the interactions, dimensional organization, temporal conditions, and parameter relations also have developmental origins. Each answer must identify what actually changes. A history of abundance, a history of an interaction, and a history of spacetime impose different demands on the account.[14]

The proposed connection is cumulative. A physical differentiation can alter subsequent interactions; the resulting organization can stabilize some configurations and change the pathways available to others. Some developments can also create or maintain gradients through which further transformations occur. The theory therefore seeks a history in which asymmetries, accessible pathways, and the conditions driving assembly develop together. It must determine which asymmetries perform this energetic role: an unequal population or a preferred handedness is not by itself a quantified thermodynamic gradient.

Consider left and right. Parity concerns whether the laws remain unchanged under a spatial reflection. A particular asymmetric object does not establish parity violation: a right-handed screw can exist in a world whose laws also permit its mirror image. The issue is whether the physical process itself respects the reflected description. In 1957, Chien-Shiung Wu and her collaborators reported an experimental violation of parity in cobalt-60 beta decay.[15]

That result is different from a symmetric magnetic model acquiring a particular orientation. In one case, the question concerns the symmetry of the interactions; in the other, a state fails to display a symmetry allowed by the model. Calling both a preference of nature would conceal the difference we need to understand.

The matter–antimatter problem adds another distinction. Charge conjugation exchanges particles with their antiparticles; combining that operation with spatial reflection gives CP. Experiments have shown that CP is not an exact symmetry of all particle processes. The 1964 neutral-kaon work of Christenson, Cronin, Fitch, and Turlay was a landmark in establishing this. Yet the existence of CP violation is not, on its own, a complete account of the cosmic excess of matter.[16]

The research has continued into particles of the class that includes protons and neutrons. In 2025, the LHCb collaboration reported CP violation in a particular decay of the beauty baryon Λ_b⁰ and its antimatter counterpart. The comparison accounted for differences in production and detection before identifying an asymmetry in the decay probabilities.[17] It established unequal behavior in specified decay routes, rather than a difference in total lifetimes or the production of the cosmic matter surplus from equal initial populations.

To see the additional problem, consider the bookkeeping of pair creation and annihilation. Creating one baryon and one antibaryon increases both counts equally. Annihilating a pair reduces both equally. Those operations alone preserve their difference. If the initial net number is zero, simply repeating them cannot turn it into a net excess of baryons. A local fluctuation or a separation into different regions also leaves a global conserved difference unchanged.

Baryogenesis asks how a net excess could be generated dynamically. Under the standard assumptions of the Sakharov account, this requires processes that violate baryon-number conservation, violations of C and CP symmetry, and departure from thermal equilibrium.[16] The first permits the net quantity to change; the second permits a preference between corresponding processes; the third allows an asymmetry to develop outside the equilibrium cancellation. Their presence is a set of requirements, not a calculation of the observed surplus.

The role of disequilibrium makes this research especially relevant to the book. The early universe supplies a changing physical setting in which rates of expansion and interaction matter. An initially balanced abundance need not be the final one if the necessary processes operate. But departure from equilibrium and maximization of entropy production are different claims. The latter must still identify a measurable tendency and show how it generates the required abundance through specified interactions.

There is also a question about spatial distribution. Perhaps the observable excess is local and distant regions contain compensating antimatter. That possibility resembles the two-compartment illustration only superficially: matter and antimatter can annihilate where they meet. In their study of a cosmological patchwork, Andrew Cohen, Álvaro De Rújula, and Sheldon Glashow calculated the consequences of annihilation near domain boundaries. Their predicted gamma-ray signal constrained such a patchwork under the cosmological assumptions they investigated.[18]

This turns distribution into an observable question. A proposed separation has to explain the radiation its boundaries would produce, as well as the matter found in the region we inhabit. The calculation is not a census of everything beyond the observable universe. It shows why moving the missing antimatter elsewhere cannot, by itself, complete the explanation.

The observed number of large spatial dimensions poses a further question. We navigate three independent spatial directions, while physical time plays a different role in our descriptions. It is possible to ask whether this organization is fundamental, whether additional dimensions might be inaccessible at ordinary scales, or whether dimensionality could arise in a deeper theory. Asking those questions does not show that a process of assembly has selected three dimensions.

Even a proposed argument about the stability of structures in other dimensions has assumptions. Which laws are carried into the alternative world? How do interactions change with geometry? What counts as a stable structure? Changing one feature while freezing every other feature can be a useful investigation, but the resulting counterfactual belongs to a model. It is not a survey of all possible universes.

Time presents an even more intimate asymmetry. We remember an opened door, not the opening that has yet to happen. Heat spreads from a hot object into cooler surroundings; we do not ordinarily see the dispersed energy spontaneously reassemble the original temperature difference. Many everyday processes have a direction even when the microscopic equations used to model them permit reversed motions.

A simple gas illustrates the statistical idea. Confine it to one side of a container and remove the partition. For ordinary macroscopic conditions, it spreads. There are vastly more microscopic arrangements compatible with a roughly even distribution than with every molecule crowded back into the original side. Boltzmann's account relates entropy to the multiplicity of microscopic states compatible with a macroscopic description. The initial condition is essential: we began with the gas confined.[19]

This is not a failure of the molecules to retrace their steps because they have forgotten the past. It is a statement about microscopic possibilities, typical behavior, and the conditions under which we began the comparison. Nor does microscopic chaos alone explain the direction. A model can be highly sensitive to initial conditions without that sensitivity selecting which temporal direction carries the low-entropy boundary.

The explanation therefore returns us to an inheritance. Why was there a partition? Who or what prepared the confined gas? In the laboratory we can follow the preparation into the work performed by an apparatus and into its environment. In cosmology the corresponding question reaches toward the universe's low-entropy past. A statistical account of relaxation does not, by itself, explain the origin of that past.

It is also not enough to say that the early universe was hot. Temperature is not entropy, and gravity complicates the inference from visual uniformity to maximal disorder. We cannot identify the entropy of the whole gravitational world merely by looking at the thermal character of one component. The problem of the initial condition remains a physical problem, not a defect to be removed by an evocative word.

For this book, thermodynamics supplies both possibility and restraint. Local organization can be maintained in an open system exchanging energy and matter with its surroundings. But entropy, energy, and power are different quantities. Energy has units of joules; power has units of joules per second; thermodynamic entropy has units of joules per kelvin. A claim about one cannot be transferred to another merely because each appears in a discussion of heat.[20]

A reservoir can hold a great deal of usable energy while the rate at which a particular system accesses it remains small. A barrier can prevent a transformation, even when the final state would be energetically favorable. Conversely, an increased rate of energy use can be spent wastefully. None of these possibilities contradicts conservation of energy or supplies a universal scale of progress.

Suppose a device depends on a temperature difference. Removing that difference can destroy its ability to do useful work even though the energy has not vanished. The capacity belonged to a relation between parts of the system. The history of establishing, exploiting, and dissipating that relation matters.

The cosmological hypothesis extends the creation cycle to the formation of its own conditions. It must explain both the exploitable gradient and the constraints, identify the next level's new energy access, and derive the recurring organization through productive dynamics. Its further development must exhibit the three dialectical relations rather than merely acquire the label dialectical. A natural history has to establish these connections before it can claim that the complete cycle explains physical origins.

That extends the theory beyond explaining what an engine does after someone supplies its reservoirs. The origins of the reservoirs, gradients, and constraints become part of the same inquiry. At cosmic scales, the hypothesis must specify which physical difference drives which transformation, which alternatives are accessible, and what entropy-production rate is being compared. The restricted principles discussed in chapter 2 do not already establish this universal extension.[20]

Symmetry breaking alone cannot establish disequilibrium. A system can have a state with less symmetry and still be in thermodynamic equilibrium.[5] The creation cycle therefore requires an account of how usable gradients arise, how an assembly accesses them, and how that access affects continuation. The stronger maximum-rate claim adds a comparison with competing pathways. The low-entropy past remains among the conditions to explain. These requirements give the historical proposal a way to fail as well as a direction to pursue.

The history of formation and the activity of a surviving product remain different objects of explanation. A stable atom, a star exchanging energy, and an operating engine cannot be assigned the same regenerative mechanism merely because each has a history. The book's stronger requirement directs the inquiry to the productive system in which the relevant organization recurs. That boundary and its energy flows must be established before autopoiesis can serve as an explanation; an isolated persistent remnant is insufficient.

The granular result is a controlled laboratory experiment, not a natural experiment assigned by an external historical event. The published research supplies evidence for a particular instability; this manuscript has not reanalyzed its raw measurements. Its evidence record therefore separates the reported mechanism from a new estimate. A test of the proposed entropy-production maximum would additionally need measurements of the complete system and accessible alternatives.[21]

The sequence has moved from the proposed emergence of definite structure to physical mechanisms that differentiate it. A differentiated state can then alter the possibilities available to later events. We can now ask whether some conditions treated as fixed laws or constants could themselves be products of this cumulative history. Fine-tuning belongs to that same question: how do mutually compatible conditions arise together?

Put a coin in a bowl and step back until the rim hides it. Keep your head still while someone adds water. From a suitable position, the coin comes back into view. The coin has not moved. The water has changed the route by which light from it can reach your eye.

The demonstration is simple enough to perform in a kitchen. Its explanation opens a much larger problem. A change in the material between two places changes the path connecting them. What appears to be a property of the route belongs partly to the environment through which the route passes.

In the language of geometrical optics, light bends when it passes obliquely between materials in which it travels at different speeds. Fermat's principle describes the relevant path by comparing travel times along nearby alternatives. For a simple crossing between air and water, the physical route minimizes the time between fixed endpoints. More generally, the precise requirement is stationarity: a sufficiently small change of path produces no first-order change in travel time.[22]

That last distinction deserves a little patience. At the bottom of a smooth valley, a small step in either direction changes height less rapidly than a step on the slope. The immediate rate of change is zero. A hilltop also has that property. Stationarity concerns this local condition, which need not identify the lowest value available anywhere. The familiar expression “least time” is memorable, but its apparent simplicity can conceal what the principle says.

For the bowl, compare two imagined routes between the coin and the same point above the water. One might use a shorter stretch in air but a longer stretch in water. Another does the reverse. Each travel time depends on both distances and speeds. The shortest distance and the shortest time need not describe the same route.

We can calculate such routes by varying the crossing point at the surface. Nothing about that calculation requires the light to make the comparison consciously. The alternatives belong to our description of the problem. The material world supplies the interactions whose behavior the description captures.

The book began by asking what constraints make possible and what maintains them. Here is a small answer. Some constraints belong to the arrangement of other things. The surface of the water is one such arrangement. It was absent before the water entered the bowl. It can be moved, disturbed, or removed. The law used to explain the bending did not have to be invented when the bowl was filled, but this particular condition of the light's passage did.

A constraint can be real without being eternal.

There are therefore at least two questions hidden inside the question about origins. How does an environment acquire a condition that affects later events? And why are there laws under which environments and events behave as they do? An answer to the first may leave the second untouched. It can still explain something important.

Physics repeatedly makes progress by finding that what was treated as a condition at one level can be described as a process at another. The danger is to stop too soon and call every condition fundamental. The opposite danger is to announce that all conditions have been explained because one of them has a history.

The principle of stationary action provides a useful discipline. In a familiar class of mechanical problems, action is the time integral of kinetic energy minus potential energy. The physical trajectory makes the first-order change in this quantity vanish under small variations that preserve the specified endpoints. For those systems, this condition gives the same equations of motion as the familiar force-based account.[23]

Action is not an amount of usable energy waiting to be extracted. It has units of energy multiplied by time. Its stationarity says nothing, by itself, about the fastest route to a more productive civilization. Nor is the physical trajectory necessarily the global minimum of action. Importing “least” into an argument about history without importing the defined quantity, the allowed variations, and the boundary conditions leaves only the sound of a physical principle.

Yet the achievement of the principle should not be diminished. The same motion can be understood through local changes and through a condition applied to a whole path. A description that sounds purposeful can be mathematically connected to one that does not invoke purpose at all.

Before we ask whether physical constants could have histories, we need one more distinction: a law can be reliable without being the deepest available description.

The stiffness of a material is a measurable property. It constrains how the material responds to force. But it belongs to an organized collection of constituents, not to a single isolated atom carrying a tiny copy of the material's rigidity. Change the arrangement or conditions and the collective response can change. A calculation at the scale of the whole material can be useful without tracking every microscopic motion.

Effective theories formalize the possibility of working at a specified scale while retaining the influence of smaller-scale processes in suitable parameters and interactions. Their success is not a declaration that the ignored details do not exist. It depends on understanding which effects remain important for the question being asked. Polchinski's account of effective field theory makes this discipline explicit.[24]

This gives a precise alternative to treating every stable regularity as an isolated commandment. Some regularities belong to a regime. They can be explained through the structure and scale at which they arise. Their dependence on a deeper account does not make them illusory.

But changing the scale of a description is not the same event as changing a law over historical time. The scale dependence of a parameter in a physical calculation does not by itself establish that its measured value has drifted through cosmic history. Likewise, a new collective property of matter does not prove that the most fundamental interactions were assembled from earlier ones. These are different extensions, with different evidence required.

This brings us to the fine-tuning debate.

In a common form of the argument, a model contains parameters whose values affect whether familiar complex structures can exist. Vary a parameter, or several, and examine the consequences. Perhaps a reaction no longer occurs in the same way, a bound structure becomes unstable, or the history of cosmic expansion changes so that certain structures cannot form. The apparent sensitivity motivates the question: why are the values compatible with the world we observe?

A probability requires more than a range of numbers on a page. We need a distribution, a reason for choosing it, and an account of which quantities can vary independently. Assigning equal weight to equal intervals of a quantity does not generally assign equal weight to equal intervals of its logarithm. A sensitivity study can be informative even when there is no agreed physical mechanism supplying the distribution of possible values. The two tasks should not be confused.

An elementary example shows why independence matters. Imagine two quantities that must be nearly equal for a device to work. If each is independently set somewhere in a broad range, close agreement occupies only a narrow region of the possibilities. But suppose a linkage forces the second quantity to follow the first. Agreement can then occur throughout a large range of settings. What looked like two fortunate choices becomes one relation requiring an explanation.

The example does not demonstrate that physical constants are linked in the needed way. It shows what a developmental proposal would have to discover. A correlation cannot simply be announced because it would solve the problem. The linkage must exist, and its operation must account for the required relation without hiding the same unexplained precision elsewhere.

Dynamical-systems reasoning changes what a successful test would look like. Instead of asking only how many settings permit a familiar outcome, ask which earlier states the proposed dynamics actually carries toward it. A stable outcome can attract a range of histories because departures produce restoring changes. The breadth of that range, the time needed to arrive, and sensitivity to disturbances can all be investigated in a specified model. Calling such a range a basin of attraction does not establish its size or probability; it identifies the work the explanation must perform. A model of assembled constants would need to show this behavior for the relevant physical relations.[25]

The cosmological constant makes the demand especially severe. In general relativity, a constant term in the gravitational equations can be represented as a contribution with the character of uniform vacuum energy. A positive value can produce accelerated cosmic expansion. The supernova studies published by the Riess and Perlmutter teams in the late 1990s supplied major evidence for acceleration.[26]

The observation of acceleration and the explanation of its cause remain different questions. A cosmological constant is a possible description of the accelerating component; discussing its theoretical problem does not establish that all dark-energy behavior has been settled. The issue relevant here is why a vacuum contribution compatible with cosmology is so small compared with scales suggested by particle physics.

Quantum fields and other contributions enter the vacuum-energy accounting. The challenge is not simply that one rough calculation prints a spectacularly large number. It is how a small net gravitational effect can remain small when the theory includes much larger contributions and corrections. The often-repeated numerical disparity depends on the calculation and its assumptions; it should not be treated as a unique, context-free prediction of quantum mechanics.[27]

A proposed developmental explanation must face that accounting. If a field changes so as to reduce one contribution, what determines its stopping point? If contributions cancel, what maintains the cancellation when other physics changes? If a mechanism drives the result toward zero, does it also explain a small value compatible with the observed expansion? Calling the outcome stable answers none of those questions until the stability is derived.

Observer selection offers a different kind of explanation. We cannot observe our environment from a place in which no observers can arise. This conditions the observations available to us. In a 1987 argument, Steven Weinberg investigated an anthropic bound associated with the formation of gravitationally bound structures: an excessively large positive cosmological constant could prevent such structures from forming under the conditions considered.[28]

If many environments are proposed, their distribution still needs an account. If only one environment is considered, the fact that we could not inhabit an incompatible one does not identify the mechanism that gave this one its properties. Observer selection, dynamical explanation, and the description of an initial condition answer related but different questions. None should win by silently adopting the question best suited to it.

The developmental alternative pursued in this book begins with ontological uncertainty and asks how definite conditions emerge through constrained physical activity. Its proposed sequence connects the establishment of relations, the formation of gradients and pathways, and the retention of organizations that change subsequent possibilities. The eventual result might be less sensitive to some earlier differences than a picture of independently selected settings suggests. Explaining this convergence requires a mechanism; uncertainty alone does not supply an entropy-production law.

Five strands can now connect that foundation to a material account of fine-tuning. Each concerns how a process establishes or preserves conditions for another process. Their combination is the book's physical proposal for how an ontologically uncertain reality acquires enduring, mutually compatible structure.

First, constraints can progressively narrow the accessible configurations along a developmental history. Once a boundary, interaction, or stable relation exists, some continuations become unavailable and others become much easier to reach. A mathematical inventory of independently adjustable settings may then count combinations the physical process could never produce. Narrowing concerns the continuations available under those conditions; an assembly can also open new possibilities that were previously inaccessible. The claim is about the organization of accessible paths, not a universal shrinking of every physical state space.

Second, least-time and, more generally, stationary-action principles show how physical trajectories can obey precise relations without an agent comparing every imaginable route. In their applicable settings, these principles express the dynamics together with specified conditions. They give the proposed synthesis a model of physically realized constraint, while leaving the origin of the action and its parameters to be explained. Stationarity itself does not select a unique universe or establish a maximum rate of entropy production.[23]

Third, the symmetry-breaking mechanisms examined earlier show how an available range of behavior can acquire persistent structure. The resulting state becomes a condition of subsequent events. Extending this explanation to parameter values requires identifying which properties are determined, which remain contingent, and what selects among the possible states.[29]

Fourth, recursively self-maintaining organization can preserve conditions that a transient configuration would lose. Autopoiesis supplies the demanding example in which operations regenerate the organization that makes those operations possible. The proposed cosmological extension would identify corresponding dependencies: which processes maintain which structures, and how those structures sustain further processes. Stable persistence is only the beginning of that explanation. The recursive relationships and their physical support must be specified before they can explain the continuation of a cosmic organization.

Fifth, accumulated structures change the conditions for subsequent structures. Development can retain an achievement and use it in a later transformation. The next event therefore occurs within an organization partly produced by earlier events. In the fine-tuning hypothesis, some relations that appear remarkably coordinated when viewed as independent settings would instead be connected outcomes of this cumulative history.

Taken together, these mechanisms suggest a plausible path by which fine-tuning could have been a material and practical process: transformations occurring through available interactions, stabilizing some outcomes, maintaining some of their conditions, and making further transformations accessible. Here practical means physically achievable and capable of continuing under its actual conditions. The proposed explanation must eventually account for rates, barriers, stability, and the resources required for each transition.

This challenges the inference that peculiar constants make the universe intrinsically unlikely. Peculiarity alone supplies neither a probability distribution nor evidence that the constants were independently chosen. A successful developmental model could overturn an improbability estimate based on those assumptions by showing how the relevant relations are physically generated. The mechanisms assembled here motivate that alternative; they have not already derived the observed constants or resolved every physical fine-tuning problem.[30]

There are concrete proposals in theoretical physics that give this kind of question mathematical form. In 2015, Peter Graham, David Kaplan, and Surjeet Rajendran proposed a mechanism in which early-universe dynamics could help establish the electroweak scale. An evolving field changes the effective Higgs mass parameter; the development of a Higgs vacuum value affects barriers that can stop the field's motion. Their proposal is often called the relaxion mechanism.[31]

This is a proposed solution to a particular hierarchy problem, not an experimentally established history of the universe. It introduces additional physics and demanding cosmological conditions. It does not solve the cosmological constant problem merely because it concerns a scale set through cosmic evolution. Its relevance here is methodological: it specifies changing quantities, interactions, and a stopping process. A developmental claim has become something whose assumptions and consequences can be examined.

But a field settling into a stable state is not thereby an organism producing its own boundary. Nor does ordinary persistence establish autopoiesis. If we say that the universe maintains itself, we must identify what is produced, which organization is maintained, and what distinguishes the claim from the fact that physical processes continue. Otherwise the biological comparison supplies a feeling of explanation while assuming the organization whose origin is in question.

The strongest objection is circularity. Any developmental model already contains some rules under which development occurs. If those rules require just the parameters we hoped to explain, the problem has merely changed address. If the mechanism needs a specially prepared starting point, its explanatory gain depends on whether that preparation is less demanding or independently justified. The initial indeterminacy cannot be supplied with an elaborate hidden organization that already contains the relations the model claims to produce.

Yet the existence of assumptions does not make every developmental explanation empty. Explaining one quantity through relations among others can be real progress. Deriving an outcome across a wider range of initial conditions can be real progress. Finding a new observable consequence can be real progress. The standard is whether an explanation reduces an identifiable problem and survives investigation, not whether it begins by explaining why anything at all exists.

Return to the bowl. The light's path depends on the water, and the water's presence has a history. We can explain that dependence without explaining why physical law exists. We can then investigate the material that refracts the light, the organization of its constituents, and the conditions that made that organization possible. Each successful step replaces part of a presumed background with an account of how things relate.

The return to the bowl gives the entire argument a shape. Material organization changes which formal route is realized. An earlier physical history made that organization available. A mind can investigate the relation, preserve it in a theory, and use it to build another instrument. An artificial system may eventually participate in extending that inquiry. Each achievement becomes a condition of another.

The available historical constraint concerns a much narrower issue. Oklo's ancient natural reactors constrain certain proposed later changes in the fine-structure constant under stated nuclear-model assumptions. The research script records a published bound; it does not fit raw isotope data or infer the origin of constants. Stability after formation can constrain a model that predicts subsequent drift while leaving its formation mechanism unresolved.[32]

The argument now has a continuous form. Ontological uncertainty supplies its foundation. Definite relations constrain subsequent formation; symmetry breaking can alter the physical conditions of that formation; stable or self-maintaining structures become supports for further assembly. Once usable gradients and regenerative operations exist, the book proposes a preference for assemblies whose greater energy extraction supports their continuation and reproduction. Extending that tendency to cosmic origins requires deriving those prerequisites. It cannot assume a universe drawing fuel from an external environment.

This foundation also changes the book's view of intelligence. The scientific mind belongs to a world whose organization is historically produced. Its representations, instruments, and discoveries are further assemblies within that world. Our capacity to ask about physical origins has its own material history.

Early life offers a narrower comparison within the universe. Chapter 3 proposes that earlier assemblies supply components, usable gradients, and formal constraints that make the next organization accessible. What looks like a search through every imaginable configuration may instead be development along a restricted set of physical pathways. Some restrictions belong to relations we do not directly see: the Platonic side of the joint system. The hypothesis predicts prompt formation once the supporting hierarchy and conditions are sufficient for the transition. The chronology motivates that proposal; it neither establishes an instantaneous origin nor demonstrates that the universe's laws were assembled for that outcome. Smith and Morowitz supply a proposed geochemical setting for this connection, developed in chapter 3.[33]

Higher assemblies in this cosmological proposal are structures built through dependencies on earlier structures. The phrase does not identify a universal ladder on which every later object draws more power than every earlier object. The energetic prediction concerns specified successive organizations and their access to gradients. A model of assembled laws must first identify such a comparison and derive the dynamics; the fact that later life can exploit a gradient does not explain why that gradient or its governing relations exist.

The next chapter begins that human history through cooperation, care, restraint, and learning. We will follow how communities change the conditions under which their members develop, then how collective knowledge, economic self-production, self-modeling, and AI continue to reorganize what becomes possible.

